WEBVTT

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:03.060
Sawaki-kun, you knew about it?

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:04.640
About what?

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:05.790
The opening!

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.700
Did Yuuki-kun leave through there?

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:08.320
Huh?

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.780
Hey, how do you open this wall?

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:14.950
You know...

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:19.950
Sometimes I feel like I'm being 
kept in the dark about something.

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:24.580
That kind of thing really makes me mad.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.010
A. oryzae

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:31.350
Tadayasu, are you scared?

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.880
You aren't going to 
tell Oikawa about us?

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.260
S. cerevisiae

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.610
We'll seal her lips!

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:37.510
I've decided.

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:39.310
Once I'm off duty tonight,

00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:41.140
you're going to accompany me.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:42.130
What?!

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:43.750
Here's your chance!

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:44.960
Go for it!

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:46.440
<i>This is the son</i>

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.610
<i>of a family who runs a business 
producing mold-starters,</i>

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:50.930
<i>who started attending agricultural 
university this spring,</i>

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:53.440
<i>Sawaki Souemon Tadayasu-kun.</i>

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:57.600
<i>He has the ability to see 
microbes with the naked eye.</i>

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:58.990
<i>Amazing, isn't it?</i>

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:06.460
Moyashimon

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:06.460
Returns

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:35.710
Fermenting Cellar

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:32.570
So, all households used to make

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:35.710
their own miso and soy sauce.

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:38.570
Each household produced 
a different flavor,

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:40.280
truly "the taste of home."

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:42.700
Some say that must mean the dietary

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:44.920
culture in those days was very meager,

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:48.850
Near a Certain Train Station

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:46.160
Who 
would've 
thought

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:46.370
but compared to the food places

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:47.750
we 
could 
get

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:48.280
around train stations 
throughout the city

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:48.830
Ohma 
tuna 
and

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.060
that all sell nearly the same things,

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:50.120
seasonal 
Kyoto 
vegetables

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:53.180
which era truly had the 
greater variety of flavors?

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.100
for 
that 
price 
in 
Ginza?

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.230
Shut 
up!

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:55.520
Definitely the present!

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:57.430
I'd like to tell 
people from the past how

00:02:57.430 --> 00:02:58.860
delicious pasta and hamburgers are!

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:02.870
I do love miso soup 
and soy sauce, though.

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:05.620
That's wonderful...

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:09.510
Let's go to a good 
burger place sometime.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:08.240
He dropped the subject for her?

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.120
The old man did abandon that 
argument pretty quickly.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:13.630
Well, I'm off duty now, 
so I'm going home for a bit.

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:16.360
All right. I think I'll get to bed, too.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:18.880
Let's go, Sawaki-kun.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.390
S-Sure.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:22.230
Later, all!

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:23.760
Night.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:29.100
Nah...

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.950
Not those two.

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:31.910
Indeed.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:43.680
Honestly, drink coffee 
before you get sleepy.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.850
Let's just sleep for a while.

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:48.040
We've been looking stuff up all night.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:50.160
And if we go back to school and

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:51.660
straight to the library after this...

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:54.970
Searching the Internet was 
just a prelude to the library.

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:56.560
We need the university history.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:59.650
I want to know what our 
university used to be like.

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:03.870
Then couldn't you ask the professor, 
or Kei, or Hasegawa-san?

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:10.760
Ask them what that opening you saw was.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:13.080
They'll only play dumb about it.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:14.840
There's only so much 
rejection a person can take.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:18.190
Oikawa is surprisingly naïve, isn't she?

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:22.320
Well, I do believe that you 
don't know about it, anyway.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:24.810
But I hate it when people 
hide things from me.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:33.470
All right.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:35.950
I'll stay and help.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:38.470
All right!

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:40.440
You and I are going to find out

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:42.950
what lies beneath our school, Souemon!

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:45.000
Y-Yeah...

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:47.810
She called you Souemon!

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.680
Sawaki

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.680
Souemon

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.680
Tadayasu

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:49.680
Is that what they call 
a show of affection?

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:53.270
Self-sufficiency

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:52.330
Like I know...

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:57.030
Hey, about that opening 
you said you saw...

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.600
Maybe it's just an underground passage 
connecting the fermenting cellar

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:00.740
and Hiyoshi's liquor store?

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:02.700
What would be the point in that?

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.150
If I associate it with Professor 
Itsuki and Yuuki-kun,

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:08.920
I can sort of force 
myself to imagine it.

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:10.210
But the fermenting cellar

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:12.680
was originally the agricultural 
department building.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:14.420
There must be something more...

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:19.900
Horse Lane

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:17.160
Something they don't want us to know.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:19.920
So you think Kei is in on it, too?

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.750
I don't know, that's why 
I'm looking into it!

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:25.900
Gymnasium
Library
Water Purification Plant

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:25.070
I still think it'd be quicker to ask.

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:27.930
Malt Cellar 2

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:31.110
It's miso time, you moldy 
little chicken shits!

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.060
I trust you're ready for action?!

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:34.790
Mayonnaise!

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:37.900
If I catch you dawdling, 
I'll cut off your spores

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:40.570
and plop them in a glass 
of water by your bed!

00:05:40.570 --> 00:05:41.890
Do I make myself clear?!

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:43.590
Sir, yes, sir!

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:45.770
There have been lies spreading

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:47.860
around on TV that miso is good diet food.

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:50.390
But you don't believe 
that crap, do you?!

00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:52.170
Sir, no, sir!

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:55.420
Miso has always existed to supply the 
nutrient-deficient Japanese diet

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:58.640
with protein, vitamins, and minerals!

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:00.310
It is an incredibly nutritional food

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:01.950
that supplements these deficiencies!

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:05.750
I'm telling you that we're going 
to make this Japanese treasure!

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:08.300
If any of you newbies think 
this is gonna be a picnic,

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:09.790
I'll rip that ball out from your anus

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:11.590
and make miso-coated kebabs out of you!

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:13.430
Sir, yes, sir!

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:15.970
Looks like it's tough 
to be in that army.

00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:20.930
Gonna Burst!

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:18.670
He's all fired up for 
the miso preparation.

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:20.940
All he has to do is 
take it easy and wait.

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:24.940
You get that miso is an 
exceptional food, don't you?

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:26.790
Sir, yes, sir!

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:30.220
It's even been officially 
proven by science

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:33.520
that just eating miso soup 
daily keeps you healthy!

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:37.110
Eating it every day won't hurt you,

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:39.200
it can only benefit your health.

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:42.130
Miso is the hero of solid 
fermentation in Japan!

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:43.660
Sir, yes, sir!

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:45.870
Loved throughout the nation 
since ancient times,

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:49.260
its production methods and 
ingredients vary by region.

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:52.150
It is often named after the 
region that produces it.

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:53.800
It's like the wine of Japan!

00:06:53.830 --> 00:07:02.440
Grains (Rice in This Case) + Malt

00:06:53.830 --> 00:07:02.440
Gonna Burst!

00:06:53.830 --> 00:07:02.440
Soybeans

00:06:53.830 --> 00:07:02.440
Miso Barrel

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:56.590
By the way, this time we'll be

00:06:56.590 --> 00:06:59.000
making malt rice miso in malt cellar 2!

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:01.260
No complaints from any of you, right?!

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:02.440
Sir, yes, sir!

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:05.690
For Miso

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:05.290
Begin the mission at once!

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:08.450
Well, a few things add up when I

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:10.550
compare this with what we found online.

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:12.880
I'm hungry, Oikawa.

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:14.600
I'm sleepy, Oikawa.

00:07:17.330 --> 00:07:20.390
I'll give you some gum to pretend 
you don't feel all that.

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:25.070
There are lots of urban legends about

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:27.200
mysterious underground passages in Tokyo.

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:31.000
Probably because of the war.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:32.300
Lots of underground

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:34.840
military facilities were 
discovered after the war,

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:36.170
so these stories pop up a lot,

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:37.970
including these hardcore 
enthusiast books.

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:40.510
I bet there's a lot of false info, too.

00:07:40.510 --> 00:07:43.390
But it's said that universities in Tokyo

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:45.530
were turned into military 
facilities during war time.

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:46.520
This one, too?

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:50.240
It's said there was a parade ground 
and anti-aircraft artillery here.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:51.360
Scary...

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:53.490
It's weird...

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:56.000
I wonder if there was 
a research facility?

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:57.480
It'd make sense for the research labs

00:07:57.480 --> 00:07:59.450
to be used for one in 
an agricultural university.

00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:01.000
Wasn't the professor trying to make

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:03.460
Zero fighter planes fly by using 
potatoes in his younger days?

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:07.310
A research center for alternative fuels?

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:10.130
I only heard it secondhand 
from Hasegawa-san, though.

00:08:10.630 --> 00:08:16.110
Still, why would anyone try to 
make fuel from food, then or now?

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:18.390
Isn't there a saying about being 
too hungry to function or something?

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:21.550
You're just not seeing the 
forest for the trees.

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:24.950
Using American 
grains to make biofuel

00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:26.770
isn't the work of 
agricultural scientists.

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:36.280
Beans

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:36.280
Corn

00:08:27.170 --> 00:08:29.430
Everyone knew that using grains

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:36.280
In America, beans are only 
seen as food for cows!

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:31.770
as an alternative to 
fossil fuels was crazy.

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:34.470
That's why Japanese 
agriculturists have tried to

00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:36.280
There'd be more profit 
in selling them 
to biofuel factories!

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:36.280
power cars with things 
like sunflower seeds.

00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:38.280
Even South American jungles are

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:40.210
still being wiped out 
at incredible rates,

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:42.680
all for the sake of increasing 
grain production.

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:45.060
Essentially, alternative 
fuels made from foods

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:47.670
just perpetuated a vicious 
cycle the more they were used.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:49.610
American businesses are still

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:52.580
researching it pretty seriously, though.

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:54.940
To be told all this by an agricultural 
university first-year...

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:56.640
Must be a real problem.

00:08:56.910 --> 00:08:58.470
And I've said all this,

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:01.020
but we still haven't found out anything!

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:04.470
At any rate, let's take 
this one step at a time!

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:08.590
I won't forgive you for hiding 
things from me, Itsuki Keizou!

00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:11.030
Anyway, just pretend you 
don't know anything for now.

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:12.630
R-Right.

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:15.030
But you know, Tadayasu,

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:18.800
if Professor Itsuki is hiding 
that underground passage,

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:20.280
which is more scary:

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:24.760
exposing that, or telling 
Oikawa you can see microbes?

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:26.570
Your choice!

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:31.550
You're bringing this up again?

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:34.040
You said my marriage could wait

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:36.290
until I get some results 
from my research.

00:09:36.290 --> 00:09:37.840
Then, if I may ask,

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:40.470
when are these results going to show up?

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:42.930
That's not something I can decide.

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:45.910
You're saying it's something 
Professor Itsuki will decide?

00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:48.280
Well, that is a part of it.

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:50.210
I don't recall allowing that man's

00:09:50.210 --> 00:09:52.690
opinions to determine 
my daughter's future.

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:55.500
But a promise is a promise.

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:58.560
I have no intention of 
quitting school just yet.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:01.700
Then I have an idea as well.

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:09.460
That 
Night

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:09.460
Hey, Sawaki...

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:13.270
Where have you been with Oikawa all day?

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:15.760
You're both wearing the same 
clothes you had on yesterday!

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:17.010
Let me sleep...

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:19.040
Anyway, we've got some time now

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:19.430
Let you sleep? Fool!

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:21.720
until the next stage of the process.

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:21.220
We won't let you sleep 
until you spill it!

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:24.740
And we can't move on until 
Haruka-san comes back.

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:28.220
Then it'd be nice to get 
cleaned up while we can.

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:30.510
Mutou-san, why don't we go borrow

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:32.220
the student-run dorm's baths this time?

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:39.660
We're here to borrow the baths.

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:41.120
Sorry for the intrusion.

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:45.620
Y-Yeah. Just keep it clean, okay?

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:47.560
That smells so nice!

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:48.620
I know! Want to use some?

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:50.500
Baths 
In 
Use

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:52.480
Misato!

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:55.370
No! Anything but that!

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:58.130
I like you guys, but Oikawa 
and the girls are my friends, too!

00:10:58.130 --> 00:11:01.260
Drive away the villains 
Misato and Kawahama!

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:03.280
What's the use in being 
college students if

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:04.700
we let the chance of 
seeing a naked girl go by?

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:05.720
Wait, chill out!

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:08.390
Misato and Kawahama are only 
blocking the door to the baths!

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:13.400
Surely they're aware that the 
back window lock is broken.

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:15.380
This is clearly a message from Misato

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:17.360
telling us to peek in the back window!

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:28.490
Man, I'm glad we could come here.

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:28.750
C. trichoides

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:29.200
Eyebrow pencil...

00:11:29.710 --> 00:11:32.680
Big baths really are great.

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:35.460
Even though it's all guys here, 
they keep it pretty clean.

00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:38.150
They're all pretty good guys.

00:11:40.850 --> 00:11:41.750
Huh?

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:50.990
Ow...

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:51.870
What was that?!

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:53.230
What the hell happened?!

00:11:53.230 --> 00:11:55.120
Misato! Kawahama!

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:57.250
You'd go this far to 
take the girls' side?!

00:11:57.250 --> 00:11:59.040
I can't believe you'd dig this hole!

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:00.970
I don't know anything about this!

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:02.950
Then where did this hole come from?!

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:04.490
It couldn't be...

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:08.720
If Misato and Kawahama didn't dig it,

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:10.060
then what the hell was it?

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:11.260
Wait, this could be bad.

00:12:11.260 --> 00:12:13.810
What would the school say 
if they saw that hole?

00:12:13.810 --> 00:12:16.000
Let's all fill it in tomorrow morning.

00:12:18.710 --> 00:12:22.850
It's like their attempt to 
peek in never happened.

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:29.170
Oh, I forgot something!

00:12:29.170 --> 00:12:32.080
Mutou-san, I forgot my socks.

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:33.950
You go on ahead.

00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:36.590
Okay. I'm sleepy and I 
want a drink anyway.

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:37.770
I'll just do that.

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:40.900
You come with me, Souemon.

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:42.030
O-Okay...

00:12:44.090 --> 00:12:47.470
Find it! I won't say what, just find it!

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:51.040
You all know why we're back 
in this bath, right?!

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:54.350
The first-year was blond. 
You think it'd be blond?

00:12:54.350 --> 00:12:56.990
Chill out! Oikawa-chan is Japanese!

00:13:00.310 --> 00:13:03.260
This isn't a hole. It's a passage.

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:04.170
A passage?

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:07.290
It's enclosed by a brick wall.

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:11.110
They said they'd fill this in 
tomorrow morning, didn't they?

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:13.310
This is our only chance to check it out.

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:16.580
Hey, would you go borrow a flashlight?

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:22.120
Okay, I'll walk directly 
above the passage.

00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:23.940
Let's each take two steps per second,

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:26.410
in 50 centimeter strides.

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:27.880
I'm scared.

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:35.070
Huh? Toward two o'clock?

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:37.810
Hang on. Pause before 
changing direction.

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:41.650
Okay, let's go. Ready... move.

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:44.470
It's dark. I'm scared.

00:13:44.770 --> 00:13:47.030
Keep talking, Oikawa.

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:48.850
There's nothing to be scared of.

00:13:48.850 --> 00:13:50.320
I'm right above you.

00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:52.060
But it's strange.

00:13:52.060 --> 00:13:55.140
This road and the underground 
path generally match up.

00:13:55.770 --> 00:13:58.120
But if it's a military 
facility as I suspected,

00:13:58.120 --> 00:14:00.650
it seems too shallow to serve 
as anti-aircraft defense.

00:14:00.650 --> 00:14:02.550
Besides, if it was an air raid shelter,

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:05.150
it should have a more complex 
structure to stop blast waves.

00:14:05.470 --> 00:14:07.580
I don't think this place means anything.

00:14:07.580 --> 00:14:09.800
There's no sign of 
anyone still using it.

00:14:11.210 --> 00:14:13.040
How can you say that?

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:14.910
There are only soil fungi in here.

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:16.050
What?

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:17.430
Oh, uh, nothing.

00:14:17.430 --> 00:14:20.190
If people were using this, 
wouldn't they at least install lights?

00:14:20.190 --> 00:14:22.040
Ew! A puddle!

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:25.390
Sawaki-kun, stop.

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:26.800
There's a building here.

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:30.110
So it does lead here.

00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:32.300
Does the passage keep 
going straight ahead?

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:35.420
Yeah, as far as I can see.

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:38.360
Huh? Stairs?

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:41.810
If there are stairs, that means 
it does lead inside the building!

00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:49.140
Thanks, Sawaki-kun. You can head back.

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:49.990
Huh?

00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:53.040
This route doesn't lead 
to the opening I saw.

00:14:53.730 --> 00:14:57.000
But I wonder why I never thought 
this was strange before...

00:14:57.720 --> 00:15:00.010
It's welded shut.

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:02.610
Guess I'll head back.

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:08.280
She's already asleep.

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:11.140
Is Hasegawa-kun here?

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:13.150
No, I haven't seen her today.

00:15:13.150 --> 00:15:14.700
As I thought...

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:16.390
Is that hers?

00:15:16.820 --> 00:15:17.770
Yes.

00:15:17.770 --> 00:15:20.830
It was in the locker, 
so I helped myself.

00:15:20.830 --> 00:15:22.060
How do I look?

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:24.150
Quite nice.

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:28.090
Anyway, I wonder if we should 
check on the malt tonight?

00:15:28.090 --> 00:15:31.160
Maybe I'll look in on my way home.

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:33.870
Oh! Could you, please?

00:15:33.870 --> 00:15:34.720
This is great!

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:36.910
It was my turn tonight, 
so I'd appreciate it!

00:15:36.910 --> 00:15:39.410
Fermenting Cellar

00:15:36.910 --> 00:15:39.410
Sure. See you tomorrow.

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:43.670
Thanks! I love you, Professor!

00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:50.360
I found it! I found one!

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:51.430
Seriously?!

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:53.410
Hey, there's one here, too!

00:15:53.410 --> 00:15:54.490
Wait a minute!

00:15:54.490 --> 00:15:56.810
I just realized we're naked ourselves!

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:56.810
Today, I sleep.

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:09.340
Morning.

00:16:11.380 --> 00:16:13.500
Good morning.

00:16:15.310 --> 00:16:17.550
Mutou-kun went home?

00:16:17.550 --> 00:16:20.080
Huh? Oh... so she did.

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:21.450
Darn...

00:16:21.450 --> 00:16:23.820
And I was thinking of 
making rice vinegar

00:16:23.820 --> 00:16:26.710
and mirin with the barreled 
sake my friend gave me.

00:16:27.790 --> 00:16:30.430
You mean just my help 
wouldn't be enough?

00:16:30.430 --> 00:16:32.010
You might as well.

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:34.930
If Mutou-san were here, 
she'd get mad and say it was a waste.

00:16:33.710 --> 00:16:36.820
 Agricultural University 
History Report

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:37.100
"A waste" is a bit harsh!

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:39.990
Does seem like something 
she'd say, though.

00:16:40.410 --> 00:16:42.830
Rice Shochu

00:16:41.320 --> 00:16:42.850
Well then, let's get started.

00:16:42.890 --> 00:16:45.350
Water and Sake

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:48.930
Wait, so rice vinegar and mirin 
are both made from sake?

00:16:49.610 --> 00:16:51.720
But they taste completely different.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:52.950
Yes.

00:16:52.950 --> 00:16:56.560
Vinegar is said to be the 
oldest seasoning made by man.

00:16:53.860 --> 00:17:00.450
A. pasteurianus

00:16:54.010 --> 00:17:00.450
A. aceti

00:16:54.680 --> 00:16:57.970
Acetic 
Acid 
Bacteria

00:16:56.560 --> 00:16:58.380
It is loved all around the world.

00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:03.870
It's made by acetobacters that 
turn alcohol into acetic acid.

00:17:05.020 --> 00:17:07.660
Rice malt converts rice to sugar.

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:10.930
We use a liquid prepared 
by alcohol fermentation -

00:17:08.670 --> 00:17:12.050
Diluted Sake

00:17:10.930 --> 00:17:12.710
in other words, sake.

00:17:13.930 --> 00:17:16.950
Then we add this acetic acid bacteria,

00:17:16.950 --> 00:17:19.390
known as "mother of vinegar."

00:17:22.570 --> 00:17:25.170
Ah, this feels nice...

00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:27.150
Now we just leave it until it's ready.

00:17:27.410 --> 00:17:28.810
That's so easy!

00:17:28.810 --> 00:17:30.650
You don't have to stir it or anything?

00:17:31.030 --> 00:17:33.980
No, actually, we mustn't 
touch it at all.

00:17:33.980 --> 00:17:36.650
The acetic acid bacteria form 
a thin film on the surface

00:17:36.650 --> 00:17:40.300
as they change the alcohol into vinegar.

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:47.170
Time to Brew

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:47.170
Acetic Acid Bacteria Film 
(Alcohol -> Vinegar)

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:47.170
Vinegar 
(Heavy)

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:47.170
Alcohol 
(Light)

00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:42.950
Vinegar is heavier than alcohol,

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:44.920
so the contents are naturally agitated.

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:47.170
There's no need for us to touch it.

00:17:47.770 --> 00:17:51.100
The bacteria can't get into the 
alcohol or the vinegar easily,

00:17:51.100 --> 00:17:52.560
so we only need to add sake

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:55.300
to the remaining half of the 
vinegar when it's ready.

00:17:56.080 --> 00:17:58.830
So that's why the process hasn't 
been changed since ancient times.

00:17:58.830 --> 00:18:01.310
You can just leave it alone 
and it makes itself.

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:03.110
Some people say,

00:18:03.110 --> 00:18:06.740
"The first person who ever 
ate this must have had guts."

00:18:06.740 --> 00:18:08.760
The same goes for blowfish 
and sea pineapple too,

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:11.330
but fermented foods have 
that side to them.

00:18:11.330 --> 00:18:12.390
Yes.

00:18:12.390 --> 00:18:15.300
But do you think the first people to eat them

00:18:15.300 --> 00:18:16.900
were really respected?

00:18:20.830 --> 00:18:22.790
I can't help pondering.

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:26.910
What is the research that food 
researchers desire the most?

00:18:29.070 --> 00:18:30.420
I wonder...

00:18:31.060 --> 00:18:35.340
Well, I'd imagine Hasegawa-kun 
could answer that in a heartbeat,

00:18:35.340 --> 00:18:37.930
but perhaps it's a bit too difficult 
for a first-year like you.

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:41.920
We'll make the mirin next time.

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:43.880
The malt cellar is already in use.

00:18:43.880 --> 00:18:46.750
And above all, that one will 
require some labor on our part.

00:18:47.490 --> 00:18:48.940
Um, Professor?

00:18:54.400 --> 00:18:56.060
Shall I turn off the lights?

00:18:56.060 --> 00:18:58.030
Oh, yes, please.

00:19:13.430 --> 00:19:14.370
It's no use.

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:15.720
It won't budge.

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:18.470
But Oikawa says she saw something.

00:19:19.230 --> 00:19:20.220
Why...

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:22.240
Oh, Oikawa.

00:19:22.240 --> 00:19:23.790
You came just in time.

00:19:23.790 --> 00:19:26.220
Looks like there's no way to tell what 
it is if we can't get in from here.

00:19:26.540 --> 00:19:28.620
Sorry. I told them everything.

00:19:28.620 --> 00:19:30.600
They wouldn't let me sleep otherwise.

00:19:30.830 --> 00:19:32.380
We checked to see where that underground

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:33.840
passage goes in the other direction,

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:35.370
but it was a dead end.

00:19:35.370 --> 00:19:37.730
Have you figured anything out, Oikawa?

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:42.360
You believe me?

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:43.920
Huh?

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:45.980
What are you talking 
about? Was it a lie?

00:19:45.980 --> 00:19:47.490
No, that's not what I meant...

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:50.560
Then just come over here, Leader.

00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:52.750
We didn't know about 
that opening, either.

00:19:53.140 --> 00:19:54.990
We'll never get anywhere without you.

00:19:54.990 --> 00:19:57.070
I wonder if there's some trick

00:19:57.070 --> 00:19:58.800
to this wall, like those puzzle boxes?

00:19:58.800 --> 00:20:00.200
I don't know, but usually,

00:20:00.200 --> 00:20:03.250
these things are simple 
once you get them open.

00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:02.410
...you believe me?

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:09.060
Whoa, what are you doing?!

00:20:09.060 --> 00:20:10.620
How annoying, get off of me!

00:20:12.880 --> 00:20:14.950
Suddenly I'm starving!

00:20:16.130 --> 00:20:18.130
Let's go eat, boys!

00:20:18.130 --> 00:20:19.990
Shut up!

00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:52.950
Moyashimon

00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:52.950
Returns

00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:52.950
Episode 2

00:21:50.120 --> 00:21:52.950
Microbe Theater

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:52.950
Microbe Theater Returns!

00:21:51.620 --> 00:21:52.950
Returns

00:21:53.350 --> 00:21:57.790
This time, we'll tell you all about 
the king of hidden flavors: Mirin!

00:21:53.890 --> 00:21:57.790
Mirin

00:21:57.810 --> 00:22:08.470
True Mirin

00:21:58.530 --> 00:22:01.430
Mirin is actually a true liquor,

00:22:01.430 --> 00:22:04.960
with an alcohol content of about 14%.

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:06.840
I don't know about now,

00:22:06.840 --> 00:22:10.130
but the spiced sake people drink 
at New Year's is made with mirin,

00:22:08.970 --> 00:22:12.720
Medicinal Alcohol

00:22:10.130 --> 00:22:13.470
and it's commonly used to make 
medicinal alcoholic drinks, too.

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:16.440
So, what do you think 
the "mirin flavoring"

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:19.480
you see in stores really is?

00:22:19.500 --> 00:22:21.400
True Mirin

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:24.370
Mirin flavoring is made 
from a mixture of glucose,

00:22:22.190 --> 00:22:28.990
Mirin Flavor

00:22:23.320 --> 00:22:28.360
Glucose

00:22:24.370 --> 00:22:26.480
starch syrup, glutamic acid, and flavors.

00:22:26.480 --> 00:22:28.990
In other words, it's an 
entirely different product.

00:22:27.740 --> 00:22:28.990
Mirin

00:22:29.420 --> 00:22:34.330
Mirin is not just for adding luster 
to Japanese foods, like wax.

00:22:34.330 --> 00:22:38.960
Since it contains alcohol, it masks 
odors and makes meat tender.

00:22:39.850 --> 00:22:42.280
It also adds a more savory flavor 
since it's full of amino acids,

00:22:42.280 --> 00:22:45.170
and because it's sweet, 
you don't have to use sugar!

00:22:45.190 --> 00:22:49.970
True Mirin

00:22:45.770 --> 00:22:49.380
It truly is a miracle of fermentation!
